    "https://ecokedu.bamboohr.com/careers/144": {
      "title": "Instructional Services Librarian",
      "description": "Instructional Services Librarian. Department: Linscheid Library. Full-Time. FLSA Status: Exempt Position Summary The Instructional Services Librarian/Instructor oversees the instruction and reference programs, and works to further information literacy on campus, all while providing reference, instruction, and liaison services to support the academic mission of the university community. Essential Job Duties • Oversees the library’s instruction services with a focus on continuous improvement and interactive learning. • Plans, implements, and assesses the library’s information literacy plan. • Develops, schedules, and coordinates the library’s involvement with Freshman Seminar (UNIV 1001). • Oversees LibGuides Maintenance and Standards Compliance Review. • Develops instructional materials for the library. • Creates and maintains LibWizard forms for department operations. • Provides instruction on library use and research skills in classroom, online, and reference desk settings. • Serves as liaison to academic departments for research and collection development. • Collaborates with faculty on information literacy curriculum development and special projects. • Participates in university and library committee work. • Maintains current knowledge of emerging trends and best practices through professional development. • Other duties as assigned by Supervisor or designee. Omission of specific statement of duties does not exclude them from the position if the work is similar, related or a logical assignment to the position. Qualifications Both experienced librarians, recent graduates, and current students from ALA-accredited MLS programs are encouraged to apply. • Master's of Library Science • 1-2 years of experience • Experience with academic library systems, electronic resources, and instructional services preferred Licenses/Certifications • None Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Strong knowledge of principles and practices of academic librarianship • Proficient in managing managing instructional programs • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong organizational and time management abilities • Demonstrated ability to provide effective instruction and reference services • Ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students • Proficient with Microsoft Office and library software applications • Must have a strong work ethic and lead by example • Must be a professional, credible and respected representative of the institution internally and in the community • Demonstrated willingness and ability to act ethically and socially responsible • Ability to communicate in a courteous manner Physical Demands The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of the job. Constantly required to remain in a seated position for extended periods Frequently required to communicate effectively with diverse populations Regularly performs tasks that involve repetitive wrist and finger movement Regularly operates a computer and other standard office equipment Occasionally required to move about the office and similar spaces Work Environment The work environment characteristics described here are representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. This position operates in a typical office setting within an academic library environment. The work environment has moderate noise levels from conversations, and office equipment. The role involves regular interaction with staff, students, and library patrons. Supervisory Responsibility Oversees the work of experienced professionals and subordinate managers, removing obstacles within and across the organization to achieve objectives. May oversee the development of new methods, resolve issues, and regularly advise teams on complex matters East Central University, in compliance with all applicable Federal and State laws and regulations, does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, age, religion, disability, marital status, genetic information, or status as a veteran in any of its policies, practices, or procedures. This includes but is not limited to admission, employment, financial aid, and educational services. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the non-discrimination policies: Employment Services Director, 1100 E. 14th St., Danley Hall 111, Ada, OK 74820, (580) 559-5260. Please ensure you have completed the Equal Opportunity Data Form with your application. https://www.ecok.edu/employment-services/equal_opportunity_data_form_accessible.pdf"
    },
    "https://ecokedu.bamboohr.com/careers/147": {
      "title": "Environmental Data Specialist",
      "description": "Environmental Data Specialist. Department: Oka Institute. Full-Time. FLSA Status: Exempt Grade: 11 Position Summary The Environmental Data Scientist supports interdisciplinary projects across water research, land stewardship, water planning and policy, and sustainable communities. This position combines environmental science, quantitative analysis, modeling, coding, and data management to help Oka’ staff and project partners better understand complex environmental systems and support science-based decision-making. The ideal candidate will have a strong foundation in environmental sciences and the technical ability to analyze, model, and interpret environmental data using tools such as Python, R, SQL, GIS, and related analytical platforms. This position will work across Oka’s focus areas to develop models, automate analytical processes, evaluate environmental datasets, support research and field activities, and translate technical results into useful information for researchers, communities, resource managers, and decisionmakers. Essential Job Duties • Conduct quantitative analysis of environmental datasets related to water resources, watershed conditions, land management, climate, infrastructure, and other Oka’ research and community projects. • Develop, apply, and evaluate environmental models to better understand environmental processes, assess conditions, evaluate scenarios, and support project management decisions. • Develop scripts, analytical tools, and automated workflows using Python, R, SQL, or similar programming languages to process, analyze, model, and visualize environmental data. • Assist project teams with the selection, application, interpretation, and refinement of environmental and statistical modeling approaches. • Integrate environmental datasets from multiple sources, including field observations, laboratory results, GIS and remote sensing products, monitoring networks, databases, and publicly available datasets. • Apply GIS and spatial analysis techniques to evaluate environmental conditions, identify spatial patterns, develop project tools, and support environmental modeling. • Develop maps, figures, dashboards, reports, and other visualization products that clearly communicate environmental conditions, analytical findings, model outputs, and project results. • Maintain organized and reproducible datasets, analytical workflows, code, model documentation, and metadata so that technical work can be understood and used by other project staff. • Other duties as assigned by Supervisor or designee. Omission of specific statement of duties does not exclude them from the position if the work is similar, related or a logical assignment to the position. Qualifications REQUIRED: • Master’s degree in Environmental Science, Environmental Engineering, Water Resources, Hydrology, Natural Resources, Ecology, Geography, Geospatial Science, or a closely related field. • Strong understanding of environmental science principles and the ability to interpret environmental and natural resource data within the context of physical and ecological systems. • Experience analyzing environmental, water resource, natural resource, or related scientific datasets. • Experience using GIS for spatial analysis, environmental assessment, mapping, or development of map-based project tools. • Experience with quantitative analysis, statistical analysis, environmental modeling, or other computational approaches used to evaluate environmental systems. • Experience working with structured datasets and applying appropriate data organization, documentation, and quality-control practices. • Proficiency with Python, R, SQL, or similar programming languages for environmental analysis, modeling, scripting, and workflow automation. • Experience developing, calibrating, validating, or applying environmental, hydrologic, watershed, ecological, statistical, or geospatial models. PREFERRED: • Ph.D. in Environmental Science, Environmental Engineering, Water Resources, Hydrology, Natural Resources, Ecology, Geography, Geospatial Science, or a closely related field. • Familiarity with tools i.e. SQL, Python, or R for scripting and automation. • Experience applying artificial intelligence or machine learning tools to environmental or scientific applications, including data analysis, predictive modeling, pattern identification, classification, automation, or decision support tools. • Familiarity with integrating AI-assisted tools into scientific workflows while maintaining appropriate data quality, documentation, validation, and human review. Licenses/Certifications • Current FAA Part 107 Remote Pilot Certificate or must obtain certification within 90 days of employment. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to tailor messaging for different audiences • Excellent organizational skills, attention to detail, and ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously • Advanced communication skills with ability to effectively coordinate outreach initiatives • Evidence of field-based work and data collection. • Must have a strong work ethic and lead by example • Must be a professional, credible and respected representative of the institution internally and in the community • Demonstrated willingness and ability to act ethically and socially responsible • Ability to communicate in a courteous manner Physical Demands The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of the job. Regularly required to walk and traverse uneven terrain, stand, bend, or climb over natural features Ability to tolerate exposure to heat, smoke, dust, insects, and variable weather conditions for extended periods Frequently required to sit, speak, and operate field instruments or computers Frequently required to communicate effectively with diverse populations Regularly performs tasks that involve repetitive wrist and finger movement Regularly operates a computer and other standard office equipment Ability to perform fieldwork, including walking over uneven terrain and working in heat, cold, smoke, dust, insects, and variable weather conditions. Ability to lift and carry moderate loads; operate basic field equipment and vehicles as needed for site visits, monitoring, and prescribed fire activities; and use appropriate personal protective equipment. Mix of office work and frequent field visits to farms, ranches, burn sites, and partner locations in southern Oklahoma, with regular regional travel. Occasional evening or weekend work to accommodate producer schedules, field conditions, and community events. Constantly required to remain in a seated position for extended periods Frequently required to communicate effectively with diverse populations Regularly performs tasks that involve repetitive wrist and finger movement Regularly operates a computer and other standard office equipment Occasionally required to move about the office and similar spaces Work Environment The work environment characteristics described here are representative of those an employee encounter while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The work environment is a mix of office work and frequent field visits to farms, ranches, burn sites, and partner locations in southern Oklahoma, with regular regional trave. The position requires occasional travel, including overnight stays, with potential exposure to various outdoor weather conditions during field activities. Supervisory Responsibility None East Central University, in compliance with all applicable Federal and State laws and regulations, does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, age, religion, disability, marital status, genetic information, or status as a veteran in any of its policies, practices, or procedures. This includes but is not limited to admission, employment, financial aid, and educational services. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the non-discrimination policies: Employment Services Director, 1100 E. 14th St., Danley Hall 111, Ada, OK 74820, (580) 559-5260. Please ensure you have completed the Equal Opportunity Data Form with your application. https://www.ecok.edu/employment-services/equal_opportunity_data_form_accessible.pdf"
    },
    "https://ecokedu.bamboohr.com/careers/152": {
      "title": "ECU Opioid & Stimulant Prevention Grant Coordinator",
      "description": "ECU Opioid & Stimulant Prevention Grant Coordinator. Department: Brandon Whitten Institute. Full-Time. FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Grade: 5 Position Summary The ECU Opioid & Stimulant Prevention Services Grants Coordinator is responsible for implementing, monitoring and coordinating ECU Opioid & Stimulant Prevention Services Grant program. Responsible for coalition planning, reporting, capacity building, project implementation, volunteer recruitment and coordination, local media outputs, resource management, and gathering outcome data. Essential Job Duties • Provide information or training on evidence-based programs to campus, as well as monthly outreach or tabling events • Manage and evaluate educational and marketing materials • Coordinate strategic planning meetings with Project Director and campus team • Perform work within the Strategic Prevention Framework • Complete required monthly benchmarks, activities, timelines, and deliverables • Maintain a visible prevention presence on campus and build relationships students and campus departments • Host and facilitate monthly campus prevention coalition meetings • Distribute medication lock boxes and other prevention and safe-storage resources • Coordinate annual prescription drug Take Back Day and related promotion and documentation • Coordinate and administer annual student and prevention surveys • Participate in monthly state collaborative meetings and professional development • Track projects for programs • Abide by policies of BWI, ECU, and ODMHSAS • Cooperate fully with BWI and its partners to achieve goals and objectives • Other duties as assigned by Supervisor or designee. Omission of specific statement of duties does not exclude them from the position if the work is similar, related or a logical assignment to the position. Qualifications • Bachelor’s degree (preference given to degrees in education, social work, counseling or appropriate related field) • 3-5 years equivalent experience preferred • Knowledge of prevention field and research-based prevention strategies Licenses/Certifications • Certified Prevention Specialist preferred or individual willing to obtain CPS Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Excellent verbal and communication skills • Knowledge of online media • Flexibility and ability to multi-task with various projects, programs and obligations. • An understanding of prevention science • Some travel is required as well as evening or weekend work • Demonstrated ability to work independently and as part of a collaborative team • Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive student information • Must have a strong work ethic and lead by example • Must be a professional, credible and respected representative of the institution internally and in the community • Demonstrated willingness and ability to act ethically and socially responsible • Ability to communicate in a courteous manner Physical Demands The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of the job. Constantly required to remain in a seated position for extended periods Frequently required to communicate effectively with diverse populations Regularly performs tasks that involve repetitive wrist and finger movement Regularly operates a computer and other standard office equipment Occasionally required to move about the office and similar spaces Occasionally moves materials and equipment weighing up to 25 pounds Work Environment The work environment characteristics described here are representative of those an employee encounter while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The work is performed in a typical office setting with moderate noise levels from conversations, and standard office equipment. Supervisory Responsibility None East Central University, in compliance with all applicable Federal and State laws and regulations, does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, age, religion, disability, marital status, genetic information, or status as a veteran in any of its policies, practices, or procedures. This includes but is not limited to admission, employment, financial aid, and educational services. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the non-discrimination policies: Employment Services Director, 1100 E. 14th St., Danley Hall 111, Ada, OK 74820, (580) 559-5260. Please ensure you have completed the Equal Opportunity Data Form with your application. https://www.ecok.edu/employment-services/equal_opportunity_data_form_accessible.pdf"
